**Books need `rights: licensed` and `url: offline`**, with title, author and date.
This folder has more books than any other: Positioning, Designing Brand Identity,
This Is Marketing, Buyology.

**Neuromarketing and brand psychology claims are contested.** Tier them 3 unless a
source cites controlled research, and even then check the sample.

## Topics

positioning, naming architecture, distinctiveness versus differentiation, category
entry points, premium perception, brand book schema, verbal identity, visual identity,
consumer brand psychology.

## Look for

The fields a brand book must contain to be usable by an AI as context. Positioning
frameworks with a worked example. Naming criteria and the reasoning behind them. Voice
guide structure with 20 real examples rather than 5 adjectives.

## Skip

Colour psychology with no evidence, most of it is folklore. Design-process walkthroughs
about aesthetics with no positioning argument. Anything treating a brand book as a PDF
to admire rather than a system input.

## Split it in 2

Brand basics run at setup: name, domain, logo, 3 colours, 2 fonts, tone in 5
adjectives. That is enough to launch, and doing more at the start is a way to avoid
launching. The full book runs after avatars and offers, because you cannot decide how
a brand should speak before you know who it speaks to.

## Scope mix

Small baseline: the brand book exists as structured fields, not prose, and every copy
agent reads it.

## Done when

You have a brand book schema an agent can consume as context.

